D("picturesque",{etymology:E("From French pittoresque, modeled on Italian pittoresco ‘of a painter’; the adjective first described a scene suitable for painting and now means attractively like a picture.",null,"pittore","-esque","Etymonline, picturesque (https://www.etymonline.com/word/picturesque)."),contexts:[C("picturesque.s1","通用","The village’s picturesque harbor, with red-roofed cottages reflected in the water, drew painters each summer.","村莊如畫的港口映著紅屋頂的小屋，每年夏天都吸引畫家前來。","Picturesque means pretty and distinctive, like a scene in a painting.","Picturesque modifies harbor; with introduces the visual details.","The reflections and cottages create an attractive scene worth painting.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The picturesque harbor was ugly, featureless, and covered in industrial waste, so tourists avoided looking at it.","The picturesque harbor was attractive and distinctive, with cottages reflected in the water.","Picturesque describes a pleasing, picture-like scene; the first description says the opposite.")],quiz:[Q("picturesque.q1","picturesque.s1","meaning","A picturesque village is usually ___.",["attractive and visually charming","plain and unpleasant to look at","impossible to visit"],"attractive and visually charming","Picturesque means attractive in a way that could suit a picture.","meaning"),Q("picturesque.q2","picturesque.s1","context","Which scene is most picturesque?",["A stone bridge beside a clear stream and flowered cottages","A blank concrete lot filled with rubbish","A dark room with no visible objects"],"A stone bridge beside a clear stream and flowered cottages","The bridge and cottages make an appealing, distinctive scene.","context"),Q("picturesque.q3","picturesque.s1","word-choice","Visitors photographed the ____ lane lined with old cottages and roses.",["picturesque","pitiless","pigheaded"],"picturesque","The lane is visually attractive and distinctive.","word-choice"),Q("picturesque.q4","picturesque.s1","meaning","Why might a painter choose a picturesque harbor?",["Its attractive arrangement makes a pleasing scene.","It has no visible features to paint.","It is guaranteed to be empty of water."],"Its attractive arrangement makes a pleasing scene.","The adjective highlights its picture-like visual appeal.","meaning")]});
D("pie",{etymology:E("The dish name is recorded in Middle English; its ultimate origin is uncertain, though a connection with pie ‘magpie’ has been proposed because early pies contained a mixture of ingredients. No derivation is certain.",null,null,null,"Etymonline, pie (https://www.etymonline.com/word/pie)."),contexts:[C("pie.s1","飲食","Mara baked an apple pie with a flaky crust and tart fruit from the orchard.","瑪拉用果園的酸甜蘋果烤了一個酥皮派。","A pie is a baked pastry dish with fruit, meat, or another filling.","An apple pie is the object of baked; with describes its crust and filling.","The pastry encloses fruit and is baked as a dish."),C("pie.s2","比喻","The two departments negotiated over their share of the funding pie before approving the joint project.","兩個部門在核准共同計畫前，先協商各自能分到多少經費。","A pie can figuratively mean a share of money, profit, or opportunity.","Share of the funding pie names the allocation; before gives the timing.","No food is involved; pie stands for a limited pool divided among groups.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The departments divided the funding pie by baking it in a pastry crust and serving each office a slice of cash.","The departments divided the funding pie by allocating each office a share of the available budget.","In this context pie means a pool of resources to divide, not an actual baked dish.")],quiz:[Q("pie.q1","pie.s1","meaning","Which is a fruit pie?",["Baked pastry filled with apples","A bowl of uncooked rice","A glass of plain water"],"Baked pastry filled with apples","Pie is a baked dish with a filling enclosed in pastry.","meaning"),Q("pie.q2","pie.s2","context","If a firm wants a larger piece of the market pie, it wants ___.",["a larger share of the market","a bigger lunch dessert","a smaller office"],"a larger share of the market","Pie figuratively represents a pool of market opportunity divided among firms.","context"),Q("pie.q3","pie.s1","word-choice","The baker filled the crust with cherries to make a ____.",["pie","pier","piety"],"pie","A crust filled with fruit and baked is a pie.","word-choice"),Q("pie.q4","pie.s2","meaning","In “everyone wants a slice of the profit pie,” pie refers to ___.",["the total profits available to be shared","a pastry on the conference table","a type of fruit tree"],"the total profits available to be shared","This is the figurative sense of a shareable pool of money.","meaning")]});
D("piece",{etymology:E("From Old French piece ‘part, portion’, from Medieval Latin petia/pettia; its deeper origin is uncertain. English uses the noun for a part, an individual item, or a work, and the verb for joining parts.",null,"petia (uncertain origin)",null,"Etymonline, piece (https://www.etymonline.com/word/piece)."),contexts:[C("piece.s1","通用","A piece of the blue tile broke off when the worker lifted it from the old floor.","工人把藍色瓷磚從舊地板上撬起時，一塊碎片掉了下來。","A piece is a bit separated from a larger thing.","A piece of the tile is the subject; when introduces the event.","The fragment was once part of one whole tile."),C("piece.s2","通用","The editor offered one useful piece of advice: check every date against the archive.","編輯給了一條實用建議：逐一對照檔案核查日期。","A piece can mean one item of advice, news, or luggage.","One piece of advice names a single item; the colon gives its content.","Advice is treated as one discrete unit, not a physical fragment."),C("piece.s3","藝術","The quartet rehearsed a short piece by Florence Price before the evening concert.","四重奏團在晚間音樂會前排練了佛羅倫斯·普萊斯的一首短曲。","A piece can be a single work of music, writing, or art.","A short piece is the object of rehearsed; by names its composer.","In a concert context, piece means a musical composition."),C("piece.s4","通用","The curator pieced together the torn map from dozens of numbered fragments.","策展人把數十塊編號碎片拼起來，還原了那張被撕破的地圖。","To piece things together is to join separate parts into a whole.","Pieced together is a transitive verb phrase; from introduces the source parts.","The fragments are assembled into the complete map.")],mistakes:[M("word-choice","The editor gave one furniture of advice about checking dates against the archive.","The editor gave one piece of advice about checking dates against the archive.","Piece is the normal count unit for advice; furniture cannot count a recommendation.")],quiz:[Q("piece.q1","piece.s1","meaning","A piece broken from a plate is ___.",["a fragment of the larger plate","the entire kitchen","a written recommendation"],"a fragment of the larger plate","This sense is a part separated from a whole.","meaning"),Q("piece.q2","piece.s2","context","Which phrase uses piece for one item of advice?",["She gave me a piece of advice about the interview.","She dropped a piece of glass on the floor.","The pianist played a piece by Bach."],"She gave me a piece of advice about the interview.","Advice is treated as one discrete item in this expression.","context"),Q("piece.q3","piece.s3","meaning","At a concert, a “piece” is usually ___.",["a musical composition","a broken chair leg","one suitcase"],"a musical composition","The artistic sense names a work of music.","meaning"),Q("piece.q4","piece.s4","grammar","To piece together clues is to ___.",["join them to form a whole","throw each one away","paint them a new color"],"join them to form a whole","The verb phrase means assemble separate parts into a coherent whole.","grammar")]});
D("piecemeal",{etymology:E("From piece + meal, with meal in the older sense ‘part or portion’; the word describes doing or forming something a piece at a time rather than all together.",null,"piece + meal","-","Etymonline, piecemeal (https://www.etymonline.com/word/piecemeal)."),contexts:[C("piecemeal.s1","通用","Because the archive was damaged, volunteers restored the catalog piecemeal over several months.","由於檔案受損，志工花了幾個月一點一點修復目錄。","Piecemeal means in separate stages or fragments rather than all at once.","Piecemeal modifies restored; over several months states the extended schedule.","The work proceeds section by section instead of as one complete task."),C("piecemeal.s2","通用","The policy emerged as a piecemeal patchwork of exceptions, with no consistent plan behind it.","這項政策逐漸變成由各種例外拼成的零碎拼湊，背後沒有一致的計畫。","Piecemeal can describe something made or happening piece by piece, or a fragmentary result.","Piecemeal modifies patchwork; with introduces the missing feature.","The result consists of disconnected additions rather than a unified design.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The town rebuilt the bridge piecemeal in one complete operation, finishing every section simultaneously in a single day.","The town rebuilt the bridge piecemeal, completing one section at a time over several months.","Piecemeal describes separate stages or pieces, not one simultaneous operation.")],quiz:[Q("piecemeal.q1","piecemeal.s1","meaning","To repair a house piecemeal is to repair it ___.",["in separate stages","all at once in one step","without touching any part"],"in separate stages","The adverb means piece by piece rather than all together.","meaning"),Q("piecemeal.q2","piecemeal.s2","context","Which result is piecemeal?",["Rules added as unrelated exceptions over time","A single plan applied consistently from the start","A finished object made from one solid block"],"Rules added as unrelated exceptions over time","The rules form a fragmented result assembled in pieces.","context"),Q("piecemeal.q3","piecemeal.s1","word-choice","The clinic replaced its old computers ____ as funds became available.",["piecemeal","piercingly","piously"],"piecemeal","The replacements occurred in stages as money arrived.","word-choice"),Q("piecemeal.q4","piecemeal.s2","meaning","What is a piecemeal solution likely to lack?",["a unified, coordinated design","any separate components","a written word"],"a unified, coordinated design","Piecemeal changes can produce a fragmented whole without an overall plan.","meaning")]});
D("piecework",{etymology:E("A compound of piece + work; in industrial usage it names work paid by the number of units completed rather than by time spent.",null,"piece + work",null,"Etymonline, piecework (https://www.etymonline.com/word/piecework)."),contexts:[C("piecework.s1","經濟","The seamstresses preferred piecework because each finished uniform increased their pay.","縫紉女工偏好計件工作，因為每完成一套制服就能增加收入。","Piecework is labor paid according to output rather than hours worked.","Piecework is the object of preferred; because explains its financial incentive.","Workers earn according to the number of completed garments.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","Under piecework, the factory paid every seamstress the same hourly wage regardless of how many uniforms she finished.","Under hourly work, the factory paid the same wage regardless of output; piecework pays by completed units.","Piecework ties pay to output, unlike a fixed hourly wage independent of quantity.")],quiz:[Q("piecework.q1","piecework.s1","meaning","In piecework, pay depends mainly on ___.",["the number of units completed","the worker’s commute distance","the time of year only"],"the number of units completed","Piecework compensation is based on output.","meaning"),Q("piecework.q2","piecework.s1","context","Which job is paid as piecework?",["A worker earns a set amount for each shirt sewn.","A clerk receives the same salary every month.","A driver is paid only for hours on duty."],"A worker earns a set amount for each shirt sewn.","The worker’s pay rises with each item produced.","context"),Q("piecework.q3","piecework.s1","word-choice","The factory switched to ____ and paid by the number of parts assembled.",["piecework","perjury","piety"],"piecework","Pay is tied to individual units completed.","word-choice"),Q("piecework.q4","piecework.s1","meaning","Why might a fast producer earn more under piecework?",["More completed items can mean more pay.","The worker is paid for fewer hours only.","Piecework guarantees a fixed bonus unrelated to output."],"More completed items can mean more pay.","Compensation is linked to the quantity produced.","meaning")]});
D("pier",{etymology:E("The waterside platform sense comes through Middle English pier from Anglo-French pierre, probably related to Old French pierre ‘stone’; the history and relation to the architectural support sense are not fully certain.",null,"pierre? (uncertain)",null,"Etymonline, pier (https://www.etymonline.com/word/pier)."),contexts:[C("pier.s1","交通","Passengers walked along the pier to board the ferry waiting beyond the breakwater.","乘客沿著伸入水中的碼頭走去，登上防波堤外等候的渡輪。","A pier is a platform built out over water for boats or people to walk on.","Along the pier gives the route; to board states the passengers’ purpose.","The structure extends over water and provides access to a boat.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The ferry passengers walked along the pier, a platform buried deep inland with no connection to the water.","The ferry passengers walked along the pier, a platform extending out over the water.","A pier projects over water; an inland structure disconnected from water does not fit this sense.")],quiz:[Q("pier.q1","pier.s1","meaning","A pier is a structure that usually extends ___.",["out over water","deep underground","across the roof of a house"],"out over water","The platform projects over water for boats or pedestrians.","meaning"),Q("pier.q2","pier.s1","context","Where might a passenger walk before boarding a ferry?",["Along a pier","Across a dry mountain summit","Through a locked engine"],"Along a pier","Piers provide a walkway to boats at the water’s edge.","context"),Q("pier.q3","pier.s1","word-choice","The fisherman cast his line from the wooden ____ into the harbor.",["pier","piety","piecework"],"pier","The wooden platform extends into the harbor.","word-choice"),Q("pier.q4","pier.s1","meaning","What makes a pier useful to boats?",["It provides a platform extending over the water.","It is a tool for stitching sails.","It divides a school day into classes."],"It provides a platform extending over the water.","Boats can approach the structure to load or unload people.","meaning")]});
D("pierce",{etymology:E("From Anglo-French percer and Old French percier ‘pierce, penetrate’; its deeper origin is uncertain. The verb describes making a hole or passing through a surface, and also sound carrying through noise.",null,"percer (deeper origin uncertain)",null,"Etymonline, pierce (https://www.etymonline.com/word/pierce)."),contexts:[C("pierce.s1","通用","The awl pierced the leather where the saddle-maker planned to sew the new strap.","馬鞍匠打算縫上新皮帶的位置上，錐子刺穿了皮革。","To pierce is to make a hole in something with a sharp object.","Pierced takes leather as object; where introduces the intended location.","The pointed awl makes an opening through the material."),C("pierce.s2","書面","A child’s cry pierced the crowd noise and reached the nurse beside the gate.","孩子的哭聲穿透人群的喧鬧，傳到了大門旁護士的耳中。","To pierce can mean go through a surface or be heard through noise.","Pierced the crowd noise describes the sound’s force; and links its reaching the listener.","The cry remains audible despite competing sounds.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The needle pierced the fabric by resting gently on top without making a hole or passing through it.","The needle touched the fabric without piercing it; to pierce means to make a hole through the material.","Pierce requires penetration or an opening, not mere contact with the surface.")],quiz:[Q("pierce.q1","pierce.s1","meaning","To pierce a sheet with a tack is to ___.",["push a sharp point through it","fold it without touching it","paint only its outer edge"],"push a sharp point through it","The verb means make a hole with a pointed object.","meaning"),Q("pierce.q2","pierce.s2","context","A whistle pierced the station noise when it ___.",["could be heard clearly through the noise","was swallowed by silence","was hidden in a drawer"],"could be heard clearly through the noise","This figurative sense means sound carries through competing noise.","context"),Q("pierce.q3","pierce.s1","word-choice","The thorn can ____ a thin glove and scratch the skin.",["pierce","piece","pilot"],"pierce","A sharp thorn can penetrate the glove.","word-choice"),Q("pierce.q4","pierce.s2","meaning","Why did the alarm pierce the sleeping house?",["Its sound cut through the quiet and woke people.","It made a hole in every wall.","It silently changed the furniture."],"Its sound cut through the quiet and woke people.","Pierce can describe a sound that is heard through an environment.","meaning")]});
D("piercing",{etymology:E("Formed from pierce + -ing; pierce came through Anglo-French percer from Old French percier, of uncertain deeper origin. The adjective describes a sharply cutting sound or cold, or an unusually penetrating gaze.",null,"pierce","-ing","Etymonline, pierce (https://www.etymonline.com/word/pierce)."),contexts:[C("piercing.s1","通用","A piercing wind swept across the open platform, cutting through the passengers’ coats.","刺骨的寒風吹過空曠月台，穿透乘客的大衣。","Piercing describes sound or cold so sharp that it seems to cut.","Piercing modifies wind; cutting through explains its felt force.","The cold feels sharply penetrating rather than mild."),C("piercing.s2","通用","The detective gave the witness a piercing look that made him reconsider his rehearsed answer.","偵探銳利地看了證人一眼，使他重新考慮排練好的回答。","Piercing can describe a look or sound that seems to see through someone.","Piercing modifies look; that introduces its effect on the witness.","The gaze appears unusually perceptive and penetrating.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The piercing wind felt warm and gentle, while the detective’s piercing look showed no attention or insight.","The biting wind felt cold, while the detective’s piercing look seemed sharp and perceptive.","Both uses of piercing imply sharp force: intense cold or a penetrating gaze, not warmth or inattentiveness.")],quiz:[Q("piercing.q1","piercing.s1","meaning","A piercing whistle is ___.",["very high and sharp","too soft to hear","a written message"],"very high and sharp","Piercing describes a sound that seems sharply cutting.","meaning"),Q("piercing.q2","piercing.s2","context","A piercing question is likely to ___.",["expose a key point or uncomfortable truth","ask only about the weather","contain no words"],"expose a key point or uncomfortable truth","The figurative sense suggests keen insight that cuts through appearances.","context"),Q("piercing.q3","piercing.s1","word-choice","The ____ winter wind stung the hikers’ faces.",["piercing","pious","piecemeal"],"piercing","The wind was intensely cold, as if it cut the skin.","word-choice"),Q("piercing.q4","piercing.s2","meaning","Why was the judge’s gaze called piercing?",["It seemed to notice details others missed.","It was warm like a blanket.","It was a type of wooden platform."],"It seemed to notice details others missed.","Piercing can mean penetrating and perceptive.","meaning")]});
D("piety",{etymology:E("From Latin pietas ‘dutiful conduct, loyalty, devotion’, from pius ‘dutiful, devout’; English piety chiefly means religious devotion and can also mean dutiful respect for parents.",null,"pius","-ty","Etymonline, piety (https://www.etymonline.com/word/piety)."),contexts:[C("piety.s1","宗教","Her quiet piety was evident in the hours she spent caring for the temple garden and praying each dawn.","她安靜的虔誠表現在每天清晨祈禱並花數小時照料寺院花園。","Piety is devout religious feeling or dutiful respect owed to parents.","Piety is the subject complement; in the hours gives evidence of it.","The actions show religious devotion rather than merely public display.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","His piety toward the temple meant he mocked every prayer and refused to show any religious devotion.","His piety toward the temple showed in his sincere prayers and care for its community.","Piety means devout feeling or dutiful respect, which conflicts with mocking every prayer.")],quiz:[Q("piety.q1","piety.s1","meaning","Religious piety is ___.",["devotion and reverence","careless indifference","a kind of farm animal"],"devotion and reverence","Piety is devout religious feeling.","meaning"),Q("piety.q2","piety.s1","context","Which action might show piety?",["Praying sincerely and serving a faith community","Destroying a place of worship for fun","Ignoring every religious practice"],"Praying sincerely and serving a faith community","These actions can express religious devotion.","context"),Q("piety.q3","piety.s1","word-choice","The novel portrays the monk’s deep ____ through years of prayer.",["piety","pigment","pier"],"piety","The monk’s religious devotion is piety.","word-choice"),Q("piety.q4","piety.s1","meaning","In older usage, piety can also refer to dutiful respect toward ___.",["one’s parents","a machine’s fuel tank","a mountain’s perimeter"],"one’s parents","The word’s older moral sense includes filial respect.","meaning")]});
D("pig",{etymology:E("From Middle English pigge, originally a young pig; the word’s deeper origin is uncertain and is not securely explained by a known root.",null,null,null,"Etymonline, pig (https://www.etymonline.com/word/pig)."),contexts:[C("pig.s1","動物","The farmer moved the pig into a shaded pen and filled its trough with clean water.","農夫把豬趕進有遮蔭的欄圈，並在食槽裡加滿乾淨的水。","A pig is a farm animal with a flat snout and pink or dark skin.","The pig is the object of moved; and links the next task.","The animal is kept in a pen and drinks from a trough."),C("pig.s2","口語","He apologized for being a pig at dinner after taking the last four slices without asking.","晚餐時他沒問就拿走最後四片，事後為自己貪吃失禮道歉。","Pig can be a rude informal word for a person who eats too much or is very untidy.","A pig is a subject complement after being; after gives the reason for the apology.","The insult criticizes his greedy behavior, not his species.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","At dinner she was called a pig as praise for taking only one small serving and leaving food for everyone else.","At dinner she was called considerate for taking one small serving and leaving food for everyone else.","Calling a person a pig is a rude criticism of greedy or messy behavior, not praise for restraint.")],quiz:[Q("pig.q1","pig.s1","meaning","A farm pig is an animal with a ___.",["flat snout","long feathered beak","shell and eight legs"],"flat snout","The source sense describes the familiar farm animal.","meaning"),Q("pig.q2","pig.s2","context","If someone is called a pig for eating, the speaker is criticizing them for being ___.",["greedy or messy","generous and tidy","quietly religious"],"greedy or messy","The informal insult refers to excessive eating or untidiness.","context"),Q("pig.q3","pig.s1","word-choice","The farmer led the young ____ back to the barn.",["pig","pier","piety"],"pig","The animal being led to the barn is a pig.","word-choice"),Q("pig.q4","pig.s2","meaning","Why might “Don’t be a pig” sound rude at a meal?",["It compares someone to a greedy or messy eater.","It is a polite way to offer dessert.","It refers to a boat platform."],"It compares someone to a greedy or messy eater.","The informal human sense is an insulting criticism.","meaning")]});
D("pigeon",{etymology:E("From Old French pijon, from Latin pipionem ‘young pigeon’, from pipire ‘chirp’; the figurative informal sense ‘easily cheated person’ developed from the bird name.",null,"pipire","-on","Etymonline, pigeon (https://www.etymonline.com/word/pigeon)."),contexts:[C("pigeon.s1","動物","A pigeon landed on the station roof, then flew down to peck crumbs from the platform.","一隻鴿子落在車站屋頂，接著飛下來啄月台上的麵包屑。","A pigeon is a common grey bird with a small head, sometimes kept for racing.","A pigeon is the subject; then links its next action.","The bird is common in towns and feeds on small scraps."),C("pigeon.s2","口語","The con artist took the tourist for a pigeon, but the tourist checked the receipt and refused the fake fee.","騙子以為那名遊客容易上當，但遊客核對收據後拒絕支付假費用。","In older informal English, a pigeon is a person who is easily tricked.","Took someone for a pigeon means regarded that person as gullible; but contrasts the outcome.","The tourist avoids the attempted deception, despite the con artist’s assumption.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The con artist called the careful auditor a pigeon because she checked every receipt and caught the fraud immediately.","The con artist hoped the tourist would be a pigeon, someone easy to trick, but the tourist caught the fraud.","Pigeon in the figurative sense means an easily deceived person; a careful fraud-catcher is the opposite.")],quiz:[Q("pigeon.q1","pigeon.s1","meaning","A pigeon is a kind of ___.",["bird","reptile","flowering tree"],"bird","The ordinary sense names a familiar bird.","meaning"),Q("pigeon.q2","pigeon.s2","context","In an old story, a swindler calls a traveler a pigeon because the traveler is ___.",["easy to trick","an expert bird trainer","a member of the police"],"easy to trick","The informal figurative sense means a gullible target.","context"),Q("pigeon.q3","pigeon.s1","word-choice","A grey ____ pecked seeds beside the city fountain.",["pigeon","pigment","pinnacle"],"pigeon","The bird feeding near the fountain is a pigeon.","word-choice"),Q("pigeon.q4","pigeon.s2","meaning","If a scammer thinks you are a pigeon, the scammer thinks you are ___.",["likely to be fooled","impossible to contact","a type of boat"],"likely to be fooled","The figurative use refers to a person the scammer expects to deceive.","meaning")]});
D("pigheaded",{etymology:E("A compound of pig + headed; pig supplies the insulting comparison, and -headed forms an adjective for someone who obstinately refuses advice or evidence.",null,"pig + head","-ed","Etymonline, pigheaded (https://www.etymonline.com/word/pigheaded)."),contexts:[C("pigheaded.s1","通用","The pigheaded manager rejected the safety report even after engineers demonstrated the cracked support beam.","工程師證明支撐梁已裂開後，固執的經理仍拒絕接受安全報告。","Pigheaded means stubbornly refusing to listen, even when clearly wrong.","Pigheaded modifies manager; even after emphasizes the ignored evidence.","The manager persists despite clear evidence of danger.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","She was pigheaded because she reconsidered her view as soon as she heard convincing evidence.","She was open-minded because she reconsidered her view after hearing convincing evidence.","Pigheaded describes refusing to change one’s mind despite evidence; reconsideration shows the opposite.")],quiz:[Q("pigheaded.q1","pigheaded.s1","meaning","A pigheaded person is ___.",["stubborn and unwilling to listen","quick to accept good evidence","easily frightened by noise"],"stubborn and unwilling to listen","The adjective describes obstinate refusal to consider advice.","meaning"),Q("pigheaded.q2","pigheaded.s1","context","Which behavior is pigheaded?",["Rejecting a proven repair because one refuses to admit an error","Changing a plan after seeing new evidence","Asking an expert for a second opinion"],"Rejecting a proven repair because one refuses to admit an error","Ignoring clear evidence to protect one’s position is pigheaded.","context"),Q("pigheaded.q3","pigheaded.s1","word-choice","The ____ driver ignored three warnings that the bridge was closed.",["pigheaded","picturesque","piecemeal"],"pigheaded","The driver stubbornly ignored warnings.","word-choice"),Q("pigheaded.q4","pigheaded.s1","meaning","Why did colleagues call his decision pigheaded?",["He would not reconsider despite clear evidence.","He changed his mind after listening.","He fed a farm animal."],"He would not reconsider despite clear evidence.","The adjective criticizes stubbornness in the face of evidence.","meaning")]});
D("pigment",{etymology:E("From Latin pigmentum ‘a coloring matter, paint’, from pingere ‘paint’; English uses it for substances that give color to paint, skin, hair, eyes, and other materials.",null,"pingere","-ment","Etymonline, pigment (https://www.etymonline.com/word/pigment)."),contexts:[C("pigment.s1","藝術","The artist mixed a blue mineral pigment with oil to make paint for the night-sky panel.","畫家把藍色礦物顏料與油混合，為夜空畫板調製顏料。","A pigment is a substance that gives color to paint or living things.","Pigment is the object of mixed; with oil states how it becomes paint.","The mineral supplies the visible blue color in the paint.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The blue pigment made the canvas colorless because pigments remove all color from paint.","The blue pigment gave the paint its blue color on the canvas.","Pigments are coloring substances; they add color rather than remove every color.")],quiz:[Q("pigment.q1","pigment.s1","meaning","A pigment is a substance that ___.",["gives color to paint or tissue","removes all light from a room","measures a distance"],"gives color to paint or tissue","Pigment is the coloring material in paints and living things.","meaning"),Q("pigment.q2","pigment.s1","context","Which product contains pigment?",["Blue paint made with colored mineral powder","A clear glass of water","An unpainted white sheet"],"Blue paint made with colored mineral powder","The mineral coloring matter is pigment.","context"),Q("pigment.q3","pigment.s1","word-choice","The red ____ in the ink gives the label its bright color.",["pigment","piety","pier"],"pigment","The color-producing substance in ink is its pigment.","word-choice"),Q("pigment.q4","pigment.s1","meaning","Why is melanin described as a pigment?",["It contributes color to skin, hair, and eyes.","It is a tool for piercing cloth.","It is a payment for each item made."],"It contributes color to skin, hair, and eyes.","Pigments are substances that give color to living tissue.","meaning")]});
D("pigmentation",{etymology:E("From pigment + -ation, with the suffix forming a process or resulting condition; pigment comes from Latin pigmentum ‘coloring matter’, from pingere ‘paint’.",null,"pigment","-ation","Etymonline, pigmentation (https://www.etymonline.com/word/pigmentation)."),contexts:[C("pigmentation.s1","生物","The dermatologist examined the patch of darker pigmentation on the patient’s arm and compared it with older photographs.","皮膚科醫師檢查病人手臂上一塊較深的色素沉著，並拿它與舊照片比較。","Pigmentation is color in skin, hair, eyes, or tissue produced by pigments such as melanin.","Pigmentation is the object of examined; on the arm locates the patch.","The darker area results from pigment in the skin.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The pale patch showed increased pigmentation because it contained no color-producing pigment at all.","The pale patch showed reduced pigmentation because it contained less color-producing pigment.","Pigmentation refers to tissue color produced by pigments; absence of pigment means less, not increased, pigmentation.")],quiz:[Q("pigmentation.q1","pigmentation.s1","meaning","Skin pigmentation refers to ___.",["color produced by pigments in the skin","the skin’s surface temperature","the number of hairs on an arm"],"color produced by pigments in the skin","Pigmentation is the color in tissue caused by substances such as melanin.","meaning"),Q("pigmentation.q2","pigmentation.s1","context","A dermatologist compares a dark skin spot with old photos to check its ___.",["pigmentation","piecework","perimeter"],"pigmentation","The doctor evaluates the spot’s distribution and color in the skin.","context"),Q("pigmentation.q3","pigmentation.s1","word-choice","Melanin contributes to the ____ of skin and hair.",["pigmentation","pier","piety"],"pigmentation","Melanin is a pigment that gives color to tissue.","word-choice"),Q("pigmentation.q4","pigmentation.s1","meaning","What can a change in pigmentation indicate?",["A change in the amount or distribution of tissue pigment","A change in a school timetable","A new musical composition"],"A change in the amount or distribution of tissue pigment","Pigmentation describes color formed by pigments in tissue.","meaning")]});
D("pigpen",{etymology:E("A compound of pig + pen, where pen is an enclosure for animals; the figurative use compares a very dirty room or place to an untidy pig enclosure.",null,"pig + pen",null,"Etymonline, pigpen (https://www.etymonline.com/word/pigpen)."),contexts:[C("pigpen.s1","通用","The farmer scrubbed the pigpen before moving the young animals into it for the night.","農夫在把小豬趕進去過夜前，先把豬圈刷洗乾淨。","A pigpen is an enclosure for pigs; informally it can mean an extremely dirty room.","Pigpen is the object of scrubbed; before introduces the reason and timing.","The enclosure houses farm animals and needs cleaning."),C("pigpen.s1","口語","After a week of takeout boxes and laundry, my bedroom looked like a pigpen.","一週下來外賣盒和衣服堆滿房間，我的臥室看起來像個豬圈。","Figuratively, pigpen describes a very filthy or untidy place.","Looked like a pigpen is a comparison; after gives the cause of the mess.","The bedroom is not literally for pigs; it is being criticized as dirty.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The neat bedroom was called a pigpen because every book was shelved and the floor was spotless.","The cluttered bedroom was called a pigpen because clothes and boxes covered its floor.","Figurative pigpen means an extremely dirty or untidy place, not a spotless room.")],quiz:[Q("pigpen.q1","pigpen.s1","meaning","A literal pigpen is ___.",["an enclosure where pigs are kept","a place for storing paintings","a kind of musical instrument"],"an enclosure where pigs are kept","The compound names a pen for pigs.","meaning"),Q("pigpen.q2","pigpen.s1","context","If a roommate says “This kitchen is a pigpen,” the kitchen is probably ___.",["very dirty and untidy","a clean animal enclosure","under the ocean"],"very dirty and untidy","The figurative use criticizes a messy place.","context"),Q("pigpen.q3","pigpen.s1","word-choice","The farmer repaired the fence around the ____ before feeding the animals.",["pigpen","piety","piecework"],"pigpen","The fence encloses the pigs.","word-choice"),Q("pigpen.q4","pigpen.s1","meaning","Why did the parent call the playroom a pigpen?",["Toys and clothes were scattered everywhere.","The room was freshly cleaned.","It was a small platform over water."],"Toys and clothes were scattered everywhere.","The comparison means the playroom was extremely untidy.","meaning")]});
D("pigsty",{etymology:E("A compound of pig + sty; sty comes from Old English stig ‘hall, enclosure’, later a place for keeping pigs. Figuratively, pigsty means a very dirty place.",null,"pig + stig","-","Etymonline, pigsty (https://www.etymonline.com/word/pigsty)."),contexts:[C("pigsty.s1","通用","The pigs crowded into the straw-lined pigsty when the rain began.","下雨時，豬群擠進鋪著稻草的豬圈。","A pigsty is a place where pigs are kept; informally it can mean any extremely dirty place.","Into the pigsty gives the animals’ destination; when marks the trigger.","The structure shelters the pigs from the rain."),C("pigsty.s1","口語","With unwashed dishes covering the desk, the student’s dorm room had become a pigsty.","未洗的碗盤堆滿桌子，學生宿舍房間已經變成一團髒亂。","Figuratively, pigsty means a place that is extremely dirty.","Had become is followed by the comparison; with introduces evidence of the mess.","The room resembles a filthy animal enclosure because it is neglected.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","They called the spotless kitchen a pigsty even though every surface had been cleaned and polished.","They called the filthy kitchen a pigsty because dishes and rubbish covered every surface.","Pigsty as an insult describes extreme dirt; a spotless kitchen does not fit.")],quiz:[Q("pigsty.q1","pigsty.s1","meaning","A pigsty is literally ___.",["a place where pigs are kept","a shelter for racing pigeons","a tool for painting"],"a place where pigs are kept","The literal compound refers to pigs’ enclosure.","meaning"),Q("pigsty.q2","pigsty.s1","context","Why might someone call a messy garage a pigsty?",["It is extremely dirty and untidy.","It contains a healthy pig.","It is carefully organized."],"It is extremely dirty and untidy.","The figurative sense compares a messy place to a dirty pig enclosure.","context"),Q("pigsty.q3","pigsty.s1","word-choice","The farmer spread fresh straw across the floor of the ____.",["pigsty","pinnacle","piercing"],"pigsty","The straw is placed in the pigs’ shelter.","word-choice"),Q("pigsty.q4","pigsty.s1","meaning","Which description fits a pigsty in figurative use?",["A kitchen with spoiled food and rubbish everywhere","A newly painted and spotless kitchen","A quiet library with labeled shelves"],"A kitchen with spoiled food and rubbish everywhere","The word can criticize a place for being extremely dirty.","meaning")]});
D("pike",{etymology:E("The weapon name came through French pique ‘pointed weapon’, from piquer ‘to prick’; the fish name is separately recorded from Old English pīc, probably referring to its pointed snout. The histories are not fully identical.",null,"piquer / Old English pīc",null,"Etymonline, pike (https://www.etymonline.com/word/pike)."),contexts:[C("pike.s1","歷史","The infantryman carried a long pike with a steel point to keep mounted attackers at a distance.","步兵拿著一支尖端為鋼製的長矛，以便讓騎兵攻擊者保持距離。","A pike can be a long infantry spear or a long freshwater predatory fish.","A long pike is the object of carried; with describes its point and use.","The weapon’s length and sharp head help defend against mounted troops."),C("pike.s1","動物","A northern pike waited among the reeds before darting after a smaller fish.","一條北方狗魚埋伏在蘆葦間，隨後突然追捕較小的魚。","Pike also names a long predatory freshwater fish of the genus Esox.","A northern pike is the subject; before links waiting to the sudden attack.","The animal is a freshwater predator, not the pole weapon."),C("pike.s2","歷史","The soldiers were ordered to pike the wooden target during the drill, practicing the old spear thrust.","演習中士兵奉命用長矛刺向木靶，練習古老的刺擊動作。","To pike is to pierce or kill with a pike spear.","Pike is a transitive verb with target as object; during states when.","The verb means attack using the long pointed weapon.")],mistakes:[M("word-choice","The angler lifted a steel pike spear from the lake, and the fish swam away through the reeds.","The angler lifted a northern pike fish from the lake, and the fish had a long pointed snout.","In the lake context pike is the fish; a spear is a separate weapon despite sharing the word.")],quiz:[Q("pike.q1","pike.s1","meaning","A medieval infantry pike is ___.",["a long pointed spear","a short cooking spoon","a small songbird"],"a long pointed spear","The weapon sense names a long spear with a pointed head.","meaning"),Q("pike.q2","pike.s1","context","Which pike might an angler catch?",["A long freshwater predatory fish","A steel-tipped pole carried by an infantryman","A type of pastry"],"A long freshwater predatory fish","Pike also names a fish of the genus Esox.","context"),Q("pike.q3","pike.s2","grammar","In “The guard piked the straw target,” piked means ___.",["pierced it with a pike spear","painted it pink","divided it into portions"],"pierced it with a pike spear","The verb sense means thrust at or pierce with the weapon.","grammar"),Q("pike.q4","pike.s1","word-choice","The lake’s ____ lurked among the weeds before striking at minnows.",["pike","piecework","piety"],"pike","The predator in the lake is the freshwater fish called a pike.","word-choice")]});
D("pile",{etymology:E("The heap noun came through Anglo-French/Old French pile ‘heap, stack’; a separate architectural pile is a post driven into the ground. The assigned senses here are heap, large amount, and the verb to heap or crowd.",null,"Old French pile",null,"Etymonline, pile (https://www.etymonline.com/word/pile)."),contexts:[C("pile.s1","通用","A pile of clean towels waited beside the swimming pool for the children.","游泳池旁放著一疊乾淨毛巾，等著孩子們使用。","A pile is a number of things lying one upon another.","A pile of towels is the subject; beside locates it.","The towels are stacked together rather than spread out."),C("pile.s2","口語","The interns had piles of paperwork to sort before the new quarter began.","新季度開始前，實習生有一大堆文件要整理。","Informally, piles can mean a large amount of work or another thing.","Piles of paperwork is the object of had; before sets the deadline.","The plural emphasizes an unusually large quantity."),C("pile.s3","通用","Guests piled into the tiny elevator just before the theater doors opened.","劇院開門前，客人們擠進了小小的電梯。","To pile can mean put things into a stack or crowd into a place.","Piled into is an intransitive verb phrase describing the guests’ movement.","Many people enter the small space together in a crowd.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The towels lay in a pile, each one separated across a different room rather than stacked together.","The towels lay in a pile, stacked one on top of another in the same place.","A pile is a grouping of things lying upon one another, not items scattered separately.")],quiz:[Q("pile.q1","pile.s1","meaning","A pile of books is ___.",["books stacked together","books scattered across separate towns","one book opened flat"],"books stacked together","The noun means a number of things lying one upon another.","meaning"),Q("pile.q2","pile.s2","context","“We have piles of work” means ___.",["we have a large amount of work","we have no tasks at all","we are standing on books"],"we have a large amount of work","Piles is an informal way to emphasize a large quantity.","context"),Q("pile.q3","pile.s3","meaning","If people pile into a bus, they ___.",["crowd into it","leave it one at a time","build it from wood"],"crowd into it","The verb sense describes many people entering a space together.","meaning"),Q("pile.q4","pile.s1","word-choice","The cook set a ____ of plates on the counter after washing them.",["pile","piety","pier"],"pile","The plates are stacked in one place.","word-choice")]});
D("pilferage",{etymology:E("From pilfer + -age, naming the act or repeated practice of pilfering; pilfer came into English in the late Middle Ages, but its deeper origin is uncertain, so no root is asserted.",null,"pilfer (origin uncertain)","-age","Etymonline, pilfer (https://www.etymonline.com/word/pilfer)."),contexts:[C("pilferage.s1","通用","The warehouse added numbered seals after repeated pilferage of small tools from open boxes.","開放紙箱中的小工具屢遭零星偷竊後，倉庫加裝了編號封條。","Pilferage is the repeated theft of items of small value.","Pilferage is the object of after; of small tools specifies what was stolen.","Many minor thefts prompt the warehouse to secure its goods.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The shop’s pilferage policy celebrated employees for returning every item and preventing all theft.","The shop’s anti-pilferage policy aimed to prevent employees from repeatedly stealing small items.","Pilferage means repeated small-scale theft, not honest return of goods.")],quiz:[Q("pilferage.q1","pilferage.s1","meaning","Pilferage is ___.",["repeated theft of relatively small items","a large legal inheritance","a style of painting landscapes"],"repeated theft of relatively small items","The noun describes repeated small-value theft.","meaning"),Q("pilferage.q2","pilferage.s1","context","A store finds pens and batteries disappearing in small quantities each week. This is ___.",["pilferage","permafrost","piety"],"pilferage","Repeated loss of small goods fits the term.","context"),Q("pilferage.q3","pilferage.s1","word-choice","The firm installed cameras to reduce stockroom ____.",["pilferage","pier","pinnacle"],"pilferage","The cameras target repeated theft of small items.","word-choice"),Q("pilferage.q4","pilferage.s1","meaning","How is pilferage different from one major robbery?",["It usually involves repeated thefts of small-value things.","It means careful inventory counting.","It refers only to stealing a whole building."],"It usually involves repeated thefts of small-value things.","Pilferage describes small-scale theft repeated over time.","meaning")]});
D("pilgrim",{etymology:E("From Old French pelerin ‘pilgrim’, from Latin peregrinus ‘foreign, traveling abroad’; the word first emphasized a traveler and came to mean someone journeying to a sacred place.",null,"peregrinus",null,"Etymonline, pilgrim (https://www.etymonline.com/word/pilgrim)."),contexts:[C("pilgrim.s1","宗教","The pilgrim walked for three days to reach the shrine where her family had prayed for generations.","朝聖者步行三天抵達聖殿，她的家族世代都在那裡祈禱。","A pilgrim is a person who travels to a sacred place.","The pilgrim is the subject; to reach gives the journey’s destination.","The shrine has religious significance, making the trip a pilgrimage."),C("pilgrim.s2","歷史","The museum displayed a 1620 letter written by a Pilgrim who had settled in Plymouth.","博物館展出一封1620年的信，作者是一位定居普利茅斯的早期清教徒移民。","In U.S. history, a Pilgrim was one of the English settlers who founded Plymouth colony in 1620.","A Pilgrim who had settled describes the letter writer; in 1620 identifies the historical period.","Capitalized Pilgrim identifies the historical English settlers, not a generic worshiper.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The museum labeled every modern tourist a Pilgrim because each founded Plymouth colony in 1620.","The museum called the 1620 English settlers of Plymouth Pilgrims; a modern tourist is not one of those historical settlers.","The historical sense specifically refers to English settlers who founded Plymouth colony in 1620.")],quiz:[Q("pilgrim.q1","pilgrim.s1","meaning","A religious pilgrim travels to ___.",["a sacred place","a factory shift","a sports stadium only"],"a sacred place","A pilgrim makes a journey to a place of religious significance.","meaning"),Q("pilgrim.q2","pilgrim.s2","context","In a U.S. history exhibit, “the Pilgrims” refers to ___.",["English settlers who founded Plymouth colony in 1620","all visitors to a modern museum","the first people to invent printing"],"English settlers who founded Plymouth colony in 1620","This is the specific historical use of Pilgrim.","context"),Q("pilgrim.q3","pilgrim.s1","word-choice","Each year the ____ travels to the shrine as an act of faith.",["pilgrim","pigment","pier"],"pilgrim","The traveler is journeying to a sacred site.","word-choice"),Q("pilgrim.q4","pilgrim.s2","meaning","Why is the capitalized word Pilgrim used in this museum label?",["It identifies a particular group of early Plymouth settlers.","It means any person who walks to work.","It names a piece of farm equipment."],"It identifies a particular group of early Plymouth settlers.","The capitalized historical sense names the 1620 English settlers.","meaning")]});
